      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the ellipse, we start by determining the standard form of the equation of the ellipse. An ellipse tangent to the \\(x\\)-axis at \\((4, 0)\\) and to the \\(y\\)-axis at \\((0, 1)\\) has its center at \\((4, 1)\\) and semi-major and semi-minor axes equal to 4 and 1, respectively. The standard form of the equation of the ellipse is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-4)^2}{16} + \\frac{(y-1)^2}{1} = 1\n\\]\n\nHere, the semi-major axis \\(a = 4\\) and the semi-minor axis \\(b = 1\\). The distance between the foci of the ellipse is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c\\) is the distance from the center to each focus, calculated using the relationship \\(c^2 = a^2 - b^2\\). Substituting the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nc^2 = 4^2 - 1^2 = 16 - 1 = 15\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(c = \\sqrt{15}\\), and the distance between the foci is:\n\n\\[\n2c = 2\\sqrt{15}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the foci of the ellipse is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\sqrt{15}}\n\\]",
