      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by identifying the properties of the ellipse given the conditions. The ellipse is tangent to the \\(x\\)-axis at \\((4, 0)\\) and tangent to the \\(y\\)-axis at \\((0, 1)\\). This means the ellipse passes through the points \\((4, 0)\\) and \\((0, 1)\\), and its axes are aligned with the coordinate axes.\n\nThe general form of the equation of an ellipse with its major axis along the \\(x\\)-axis and minor axis along the \\(y\\)-axis is:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\nwhere \\(2a\\) is the length of the major axis and \\(2b\\) is the length of the minor axis. The ellipse is tangent to the \\(x\\)-axis at \\((4, 0)\\), so the point \\((4, 0)\\) satisfies the equation of the ellipse:\n\\[\n\\frac{4^2}{a^2} + \\frac{0^2}{b^2} = 1 \\implies \\frac{16}{a^2} = 1 \\implies a^2 = 16 \\implies a = 4\n\\]\nThe ellipse is also tangent to the \\(y\\)-axis at \\((0, 1)\\), so the point \\((0, 1)\\) satisfies the equation of the ellipse:\n\\[\n\\frac{0^2}{a^2} + \\frac{1^2}{b^2} = 1 \\implies \\frac{1}{b^2} = 1 \\implies b^2 = 1 \\implies b = 1\n\\]\nThus, the equation of the ellipse is:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{16} + \\frac{y^2}{1} = 1\n\\]\nThe distance between the foci of the ellipse is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\). Substituting the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we get:\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{16 - 1} = \\sqrt{15}\n\\]\nTherefore, the distance between the foci is:\n\\[\n2c = 2\\sqrt{15}\n\\]\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\sqrt{15}}\n\\]",
